Data: SDD

Purpose:
  Define data architecture patterns, database selection rules, data
  modeling guidelines, migration strategies, consistency models,
  partitioning approaches, retention policies, and encryption.

Owns: architecture\data.sdd
Principles:
  Choose database based on data characteristics, not familiarity.
  Schema design MUST match access patterns.
  Data MUST be owned by exactly one domain.
  Migrations MUST be backward-compatible.
  Encryption MUST be applied by default.

DatabaseSelection:

  SQL:
    description: Relational database with ACID transactions
    strengths: strong consistency, complex queries, ACID
    when_to_use: financial transactions, complex relationships,
                 strong consistency, complex reporting
    examples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server

  NoSQL:
    description: Non-relational database for specific access patterns
    types:
      document: JSON-like documents (MongoDB)
      key_value: simple lookups (Redis, DynamoDB)
      column_family: time-series, high write throughput (Cassandra)
      graph: highly connected data (Neo4j)
    when_to_use: massive scale, flexible schema, eventual consistency OK

  Selection:
    consistency: strong -> SQL, eventual -> NoSQL candidate
    query_complexity: complex joins -> SQL, simple lookups -> NoSQL
    schema_stability: stable -> SQL, evolving -> NoSQL candidate
    scale: moderate -> SQL, massive -> NoSQL candidate
    transaction_volume: high ACID -> SQL, high throughput -> NoSQL

DataModeling:
  EntityRelationship:
    rule: every entity MUST have a single owner domain
    rule: relationships MUST use explicit foreign keys or references

  SchemaDesign:
    normalization: 3NF for OLTP, denormalize for read-optimized reporting
    indexing: index foreign keys and frequently queried columns
    avoid: over-indexing, sparse columns, EAV pattern, json_everything

Migrations:
  principles:
    backward_compatible: new columns nullable/default, removed columns phased out
    atomic: each migration is a single atomic operation
    reversible: every migration MUST have a rollback path
    zero_downtime: long-running migrations use expand/contract pattern
  process: review in PR, test on staging, apply in deploy window, verify

DataConsistency:
  StrongConsistency: all reads return latest value (financial, inventory)
  EventualConsistency: reads may return stale values (social feeds, analytics)
  rule: default to strong consistency
  rule: eventual consistency MUST be explicitly justified

DataPartitioning:
  VerticalPartitioning: split table by columns (hot/cold separation)
  HorizontalPartitioning: split table by rows (range, hash, list)
  rule: partition by most common query filter
  rule: avoid cross-partition queries

DataRetention:
  policy: every data type MUST have defined retention period
  strategies:
    archive: move old data to cheaper storage
    delete: permanently remove after retention period
    anonymize: remove PII but keep structure

DataBackup:
  frequency: critical -> continuous, important -> hourly, standard -> daily
  storage: off-site, immutable, encrypted
  testing: restore test quarterly minimum

DataEncryption:
  at_rest: database, backup, file storage encryption
  in_transit: TLS for all connections
  key_management: use managed KMS, rotate annually

AntiPatterns:
  DatabasePerDeveloper: shared development DB with migration-managed schema
  NoMigrations: ALL schema changes MUST use versioned migrations
  SharedDatabase: each domain MUST own its data store
  OverIndexing: index based on actual query patterns
  NoRetentionPolicy: every data store MUST have retention policy
  UnencryptedSensitiveData: sensitive data MUST be encrypted
  PrematureSharding: MUST optimize, add replicas, partition before sharding

Navigation:
  Levels: @levels.sdd
  Patterns: @patterns.sdd
  Scaling: @scaling.sdd

State: +
